xref: Talus:bone
xref: UMLS:C0039277 {source="ncithesaurus:Talus"}
is_a: UBERON:0011679 ! proximal tarsal bone
property_value: taxon_notes "The talus is apparently derived from the fusion of three separate bones in the feet of primitive amphibians; the tibiale, articulating with tibia, the intermedium, between the bases of the tibia and fibula, and the fourth centrale, lying in the mid-part of the tarsus. These bones are still partially separate in modern amphibians, which therefore do not have a true talus. The talus forms a considerably more flexible joint in mammals than it does in reptiles. This reaches its greatest extent in artiodactyls, where the distal surface of the bone has a smooth keel to allow greater freedom of movement of the foot, and thus increase running speed" xsd:string {source="WP"}

