is_a: UBERON:0010314 ! structure with developmental contribution from neural crest
relationship: develops_from UBERON:0003117 {gci_relation="part_of", gci_filler="NCBITaxon:8353"} ! pharyngeal arch 6
relationship: develops_from UBERON:0004117 {source="ISBN:0073040584"} ! pharyngeal pouch
relationship: develops_from UBERON:0007126 {gci_relation="part_of", gci_filler="NCBITaxon:9606", source="ISBN:0073040584", source="Wikipedia"} ! pharyngeal pouch 5
relationship: develops_from UBERON:0010028 {gci_relation="part_of", gci_filler="NCBITaxon:10088", source="MP"} ! ventral part of pharyngeal pouch 4
property_value: editor_note "TODO - ensure developmental relationships correct across species. WP says pouch 4, but this isn't supported by Kardong (table 13.1 or fig 15.5). MP says 'fifth pouch in human or as the ventral component of the fourth pouch in mouse'. EHDAA2 says 6th arch mesenchyme http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16313389 says: the most posterior pouch generates the ultimobranchial bodies in most vertebrate classes, but mammals do not contain this structure" xsd:string
property_value: external_definition "Anatomical structure which includes small, calcitonin producing glands originating as epithelial growths from the sixth pharyngeal pouch.[AAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-06-20", external_class="AAO:0010547", ontology="AAO", source="AAO:BJB"}
property_value: external_definition "The glandular tissue originating from the fifth branchial pouch which combines with the buccal cavity outgrowth to form the thyroid and contributes calcitonin-secreting cells to the gland. [TFD][VHOG]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-09-17", external_class="VHOG:0001185", ontology="VHOG", source="http://bgee.unil.ch/", source="http://medical-dictionary.thefreedictionary.com/ultimobranchial+body"}
property_value: homology_notes "(...) ultimobranchial bodies that develop in all vertebrates from the ventral or posterior surface of the last pair of pharyngeal pouches. The ultimobranchial bodies are vestigial in most mammals (...).[well established][VHOG]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-09-17", external_class="VHOG:0001185", ontology="VHOG", source="http://bgee.unil.ch/", source="ISBN:978-0030223693 Liem KF, Bemis WE, Walker WF, Grande L, Functional Anatomy of the Vertebrates: An Evolutionary Perspective (2001) p.523"}
property_value: taxon_notes "In humans, the ultimobranchial body is an embryological structure that gives rise to the calcitonin-producing cells - also called parafollicular cells or clear cells - of the thyroid gland. In humans, this body is a derivative of the ventral recess of the fourth pharyngeal pouch (or fifth)" xsd:string {source="WP,unvetted"}
property_value: taxon_notes "The ultimobranchial bodies of lower vertebrates contain large amounts of calcitonin. In mammals the bodies fuse with the thyroid gland and are thought to develop into the parafollicular cells. (Stedman, 25th ed)" xsd:string

