[Term]
id: UBERON:0006633
name: coracoid process of scapula
namespace: uberon
def: "A process of the scapula in most mammals representing the remnant of the coracoid bone of lower vertebrates thas has become fused with the scapula." [http://www.merriam-webster.com/medical/coracoid%20process, PHENOSCAPE:ad]
synonym: "coracoid process" EXACT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coracoid_process]
synonym: "coracoid process of the scapula" EXACT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coracoid_process]
synonym: "processus coracoideus" RELATED LATIN [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coracoid_process]
xref: Coracoid:process
xref: EMAPA:25121
xref: FMA:13455
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/181912007
is_a: UBERON:0005913 {source="FMA"} ! zone of bone organ
relationship: part_of UBERON:0006849 {source="EMAPA", source="FMA"} ! scapula
property_value: external_definition "The coracoid process is a small hook-like structure on the lateral edge of the superior anterior portion of the scapula. Pointing laterally forward, it, together with the acromion, serves to stabilize the shoulder joint. It is palpable in the deltopectoral groove between the deltoid and pectoralis major muscles. It is the site of attachment for several structures: * The pectoralis minor muscle - to 3rd to 5th ribs. * The short head of biceps brachii muscle - to Radial tuberosity. * The coracobrachialis muscle - to medial humerus. * The coracoclavicular ligament - to the clavicle. (The ligament is formed by the conoid ligament and trapezoid ligament.) * The coracoacromial ligament - to the acromion[WP][Wikipedia:Coracoid_process]." xsd:string {source="Coracoid:process"}
property_value: taxon_notes "In humans is situated on its superior border and serves for the attachment of various muscles" xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0006634
name: lingual vein
namespace: uberon
def: "The lingual veins begin on the dorsum, sides, and under surface of the tongue, and, passing backward along the course of the lingual artery, end in the internal jugular vein. The vena comitans of the hypoglossal nerve (ranine vein), a branch of considerable size, begins below the tip of the tongue, and may join the lingual; generally, however, it passes backward on the Hyoglossus, and joins the common facial." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lingual_veins]
synonym: "deep lingual veins" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lingual_veins]
synonym: "lingual vein" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lingual_veins]
synonym: "ranine" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lingual_veins]
synonym: "ranine vein" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lingual_veins]
synonym: "vena lingualis" RELATED LATIN [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lingual_veins]
xref: AAO:0010514
xref: EMAPA:19312
xref: FMA:14326
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/181375007
xref: Lingual:veins
is_a: UBERON:0001638 ! vein
property_value: external_definition "Vein which collects blood from the tongue and drains into the external jugular vein.[AAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-06-20", external_class="AAO:0010514", ontology="AAO", source="AAO:BJB"}

[Term]
id: UBERON:0006635
name: anterior abdominal wall
namespace: uberon
def: "The abdominal wall represents the boundaries of the abdominal cavity. The abdominal wall is split into the posterior (back), lateral (sides) and anterior (front) walls. There is a common set of layers covering and forming all the walls: the deepest being the extraperitoneal fat, the parietal peritoneum, and a layer of fascia which has different names over where it covers (eg transversalis, psoas fascia). Superficial to these, but not present in the posterior wall are the three layers of muscle, the transversus abdominis (transverse abdominal muscle), the internal (obliquus internus) and the external oblique (obliquus externus)." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdominal_wall]
synonym: "ventral abdominal wall" EXACT []
xref: Abdominal:wall
xref: EHDAA:5976
xref: EMAPA:17744
xref: FMA:14627
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/362696003
xref: VHOG:0000764
relationship: part_of UBERON:0003697 ! abdominal wall

[Term]
id: UBERON:0006636
name: lumbar artery
namespace: uberon
def: "The lumbar arteries are in series with the intercostals. They are usually four in number on either side, and arise from the back of the aorta, opposite the bodies of the upper four lumbar vertebrC&. A fifth pair, small in size, is occasionally present: they arise from the middle sacral artery. They run lateralward and backward on the bodies of the lumbar vertebrC&, behind the sympathetic trunk, to the intervals between the adjacent transverse processes, and are then continued into the abdominal wall. The arteries of the right side pass behind the inferior vena cava, and the upper two on each side run behind the corresponding crus of the diaphragm. The arteries of both sides pass beneath the tendinous arches which give origin to the Psoas major, and are then continued behind this muscle and the lumbar plexus. They now cross the Quadratus lumborum, the upper three arteries running behind, the last usually in front of the muscle. At the lateral border of the Quadratus lumborum they pierce the posterior aponeurosis of the Transversus abdominis and are carried forward between this muscle and the Obliquus internus. They anastomose with the lower intercostal, the subcostal, the iliolumbar, the deep iliac circumflex, and the inferior epigastric arteries." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lumbar_arteries]
synonym: "arteria lumbalis" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lumbar_arteries]
synonym: "arteriae lumbales" RELATED LATIN [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lumbar_arteries]
synonym: "lumbar arterial tree" EXACT [FMA:14735]
synonym: "lumbar artery" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lumbar_arteries]
xref: EMAPA:19096
xref: FMA:14735
xref: http://linkedlifedata.com/resource/umls/id/C0226408
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/244285007
xref: Lumbar:arteries
xref: NCIT:C33013
xref: UMLS:C0226408 {source="ncithesaurus:Lumbar_Artery"}
is_a: UBERON:0012254 ! abdominal aorta artery

