[Term]
id: UBERON:0006650
name: tunica vaginalis testis
namespace: uberon
def: "the outer coelomic epithelial covering of the testis; a sac of serous tissue covering the testis and the epididymis within which the testis can move about; it covers the tunica albuginea" [MP:0013739]
synonym: "lamina parietalis" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunica_vaginalis]
synonym: "lamina visceralis" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunica_vaginalis]
synonym: "processus vaginalis testis" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Processus_vaginalis]
synonym: "testis tunica vaginalis" EXACT [MP:0013739]
synonym: "tunica vaginalis" RELATED []
synonym: "tunica vaginalis of testis" EXACT [FMA:19844]
synonym: "tunica vaginalis testis" RELATED LATIN [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunica_vaginalis]
synonym: "tunica vaginalis testis" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunica_vaginalis]
xref: EMAPA:19179
xref: FMA:15863
xref: FMA:19844
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/362281009
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/367713008
xref: MA:0003253
xref: NCIT:C33822
xref: Tunica:vaginalis
is_a: UBERON:0000158 {source="FMA"} ! membranous layer
relationship: develops_from UBERON:0005344 {source="Wikipedia"} ! peritoneal vaginal process
relationship: part_of UBERON:0000473 {source="EMAPA"} ! testis
property_value: external_definition "The tunica vaginalis is the serous covering of the testis. It is a pouch of serous membrane, derived from the saccus vaginalis of the peritoneum, which in the fetus preceded the descent of the testis from the abdomen into the scrotum. After its descent, that portion of the pouch which extends from the abdominal inguinal ring to near the upper part of the gland becomes obliterated; the lower portion remains as a shut sac, which invests the surface of the testis, and is reflected on to the internal surface of the scrotum; hence it may be described as consisting of a visceral and a parietal lamina." xsd:string {source="Tunica:vaginalis"}
property_value: external_ontology_notes "we follow FMA is classifying as membranous layer - but this does not account for relationship to mesolthelium (subclass or has part?). The FMA also has 'sac of tunica vaginalis', which is a serous sac.[FMA]" xsd:string {external_ontology="FMA"}
property_value: structure_notes "tunica vaginalis testis has two layers: visceral and parietal; the visceral layer lies on the anterolateral surface of the testis and epididymis; the parietal layer lines the inner surface of the scrotal sac" xsd:string

