[Term]
id: UBERON:0006946
name: efferent duct
namespace: uberon
def: "The efferent ducts (or efferent ductules or ductuli efferentes) connect the rete testis with the initial section of the epididymis. The ductuli are unilaminar and composed of columnar ciliated and non-ciliated (absorptive) cells. The ciliated cells serve to stir the luminal fluids, possibly to help ensure homogeneous absorption of water from the fluid produced by the testis, which results in an increase in the concentration of luminal sperm. The epithelium is surrounded by a band of smooth muscle that helps to propel the sperm toward the epididymis." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efferent_ducts]
subset: pheno_slim
synonym: "ductuli efferentes testis" RELATED LATIN [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efferent_ducts]
synonym: "ductuli efferentes testis" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efferent_ducts]
synonym: "efferent ductule" EXACT [FMA:19081]
synonym: "efferent ductule" EXACT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efferent_ducts]
synonym: "seminal duct" EXACT []
synonym: "vasa efferentia" RELATED [AAO:0010532, http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efferent_ducts]
xref: AAO:0010532
xref: Efferent:ducts
xref: EMAPA:18993
xref: FMA:19081
xref: http://linkedlifedata.com/resource/umls/id/C0228013
xref: MA:0000398
xref: NCIT:C32492
xref: UMLS:C0228013 {source="ncithesaurus:Efferent_Ductule"}
relationship: part_of UBERON:0006947 {source="FMA"} ! male genital duct
property_value: external_definition "Any of a group ranging from one to twelve (varies in species) small seminal ducts per testis which transport sperm from the testes to the kidney.[AAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-06-20", external_class="AAO:0010532", ontology="AAO", source="AAO:BJB"}
property_value: external_ontology_notes "Check EMAPA" xsd:string {external_ontology="EMAPA"}
property_value: taxon_notes "There are two basic designs for efferent ductule structure: a) multiple entries into the epididymis, as seen in most large mammals. In humans and other large mammals, there are approximately 15-20 efferent ducts, which also occupy nearly one third of the head of the epididymis. b) single entry, as seen in most small animals such as rodents, whereby the 3-6 ductules merge into a single small ductule prior to entering the epididymis" xsd:string

