def: "The complement of teeth in the jaws after the eruption of some of the permanent teeth but before all the deciduous teeth are absent. (Boucher's Clinical Dental Terminology, 4th ed)" [MESH:A14.254.229]
synonym: "transitional dentition" EXACT [GAID:1250]
xref: GAID:1250
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/245545006
xref: MESH:A14.254.229
is_a: UBERON:0003672 {source="GAID"} ! dentition

[Term]
id: UBERON:0007800
name: proatlas
namespace: uberon
def: "A small, paired bony element (more rarely, having fused parts) located between the skull and the atlas in tuataras, crocodiles, chameleons, and many fossil reptiles; it is uncommon in turtles and mammals (hedgehog). The proatlas apparently develops from the ossified neural part of the sclerotome. Externally it resembles the neural arch of the vertebra, since it articulates anteriorly with the lateral occipital bone and posteriorly with the anterior articular process of the atlas." [http://encyclopedia2.thefreedictionary.com/Proatlas]
is_a: UBERON:0002413 ! cervical vertebra
property_value: taxon_notes "sometimes seen in humans as an anomaly." xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0007801
name: pygostyle
namespace: uberon
def: "Pygostyle refers to a number of the final few caudal vertebrae fused into a single ossification, supporting the tail feathers and musculature. In modern birds, the rectrices attach to these[WP]." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pygostyle]
subset: organ_slim
xref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pygostyle
xref: http://people.eku.edu/ritchisong/birdskeleton.gif
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/416894001
is_a: UBERON:0001474 ! bone element
relationship: part_of UBERON:0001130 ! vertebral column

[Term]
id: UBERON:0007802
name: uropygial gland
namespace: uberon
def: "A large gland at the base of a bird's tail dorsal to the cloaca at the end of the pygostyle that secretes an oil used in preening. It opens through a caudally directed nipple." [BTO:0001420, http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uropygial_gland, http://en.wikivet.net]
comment: The gland is found near the base of the tail and is shaped into two symmetric parts. The oil of each part of the gland is secreted through the surface of the skin through a grease nipple-like nub. A bird will typically transfer this oil to its feathers by rubbing its head against the oil and then around the rest of the body. Tailward areas are usually preened utilizing the beak. Not all birds have a uropygial gland. Exceptions include the emu, ostrich, and bustards. These typically find other means to stay clean and dry, such as taking a dust bath
subset: organ_slim
synonym: "oil gland" RELATED [BTO:0001420]
synonym: "preen gland" RELATED []
synonym: "preen oil gland" RELATED []
xref: BTO:0001420
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/48626006
xref: Uropygial:gland
is_a: UBERON:0002365 {source="BTO"} ! exocrine gland
relationship: part_of UBERON:0002415 {source="BTO"} ! tail

[Term]
id: UBERON:0007803
name: preen oil
namespace: uberon
comment: The uropygial gland is involved in maintaining feather condition and secretions spread by preening. It serves a waterproofing function. The secretions contain a pro-vitamin D, converted by UV light to active vitamin D. The secretions are also believed to suppress the growth of micro-organisms, serving an anti-bacterial function. [http://en.wikivet.net]
synonym: "preen gland secretion" RELATED []
synonym: "uropygial gland secretion" RELATED []
is_a: UBERON:0000456 ! secretion of exocrine gland

[Term]
id: UBERON:0007804
name: sclerotic ring
namespace: uberon
def: "Sclerotic rings are rings of bone found in the eyes of several groups of vertebrate animals, except for mammals and crocodilians. They can be made up of single bones or small bones together. They are believed to have a role in supporting the eye, especially in animals whose eyes are not spherical, or which live underwater. Fossil sclerotic rings are known for a variety of extinct animals, including ichthyosaurs, pterosaurs, and dinosaurs, but are often not preserved[WP]." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sclerotic_ring]
synonym: "scleral ring" EXACT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sclerotic_ring]
xref: Sclerotic:ring
is_a: UBERON:0010290 {source="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/17051547"} ! scleral ossicle

[Term]
id: UBERON:0007805
name: synsacrum
namespace: uberon
def: "A region of the lower vertebral column formed from the fusion of vertebrae." [http://orcid.org/0000-0002-6601-2165]
comment: produced by fusion of last thoracics, all lumbars, all sacrals, & first few caudals;
xref: AAO:0000740
xref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synsacrum
xref: http://people.eku.edu/ritchisong/birdskeleton.gif
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/67943001
is_a: UBERON:0001474 ! bone element
relationship: part_of UBERON:0001130 ! vertebral column
relationship: part_of UBERON:0006075 {gci_relation="part_of", gci_filler="NCBITaxon:8292", source="AAO"} ! sacral region of vertebral column
property_value: axiom_lost_from_external_ontology "relationship loss: subclass portion of (AAO:0010428)[AAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-06-20", external_class="AAO:0000740", ontology="AAO"}
property_value: external_definition "Complex of synostotically fused vertebrae supporting the pelvic girdle. In anurans, the synsacrum is formed by two vertebrae and the urostyle is fused to its posterior end.[AAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-06-20", external_class="AAO:0000740", ontology="AAO", source="AAO:Pugener_2002"}
property_value: taxon_notes "In amphibians, a complex of synostotically fused vertebrae supporting the pelvic girdle. In anurans, the synsacrum is formed by two vertebrae and the urostyle is fused to its posterior end[AAO:Pugener_2002]" xsd:string
property_value: taxon_notes "In birds and dinosaurs, the sacrum is extended by incorporation of additional fused or partially-fused caudal or lumbar vertebrae. The ilium of the pelvis is attached to the synsacrum and, in some species, may also be fused to it. Together with the pelvis this forms a rigid structure which carries the weight of the hind end of the animal. In living and some prehistoric birds, posterior to the synsacrum there are usually only a few free caudal vertebrae, on the end of which lies the pygostyle[Wikipedia:Synsacrum]" xsd:string

