xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/362614004
xref: MESH:D005546
xref: NCIT:C89803
xref: UMLS:C0016540 {source="ncithesaurus:Forehead"}
relationship: part_of UBERON:0001456 {source="FMA"} ! face

[Term]
id: UBERON:0008201
name: scute
namespace: uberon
def: "A bony external plate or scale, as on the shell of a turtle, the skin of crocodilians, the feet of some birds. Scutes are similar to scales and serve the same function. Unlike the scales of fish and snakes, which are formed from the epidermis, scutes are formed in the lower vascular layer of the skin and the epidermal element is only the top surface. Forming in the living dermis, the scutes produce a horny outer layer, that is superficially similar to that of scales. Scutes will usually not overlap as snake scales (but see the pangolin). The outer keratin layer is shed piecemeal, and not in one continuous layer of skin as seen in snakes or lizards. The dermal base may contain bone and produce dermal armour. Scutes with a bony base are properly called osteoderms. Dermal scutes are also found in the feet of birds and tails of some mammals, and are believed to be the primitive form of dermal armour in reptiles. The term is also used to describe the heavy armour of the armadillo and the extinct glyptodon, and is occasionally used as an alternative to scales in describing snakes or certain fishes, such as sturgeons[WP]." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scute]
synonym: "osteoderm" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scute, PHENOSCAPE:ad]
synonym: "osteoscute" RELATED [PHENOSCAPE:ad]
synonym: "scuta" EXACT PLURAL [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scute]
synonym: "scutae" EXACT PLURAL [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scute]
synonym: "scutum" EXACT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scute]
xref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scute
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/17148007
is_a: UBERON:0007381 {quote="it is a fold in the epidermal surface hence it is an epidermal scale", source="ISBN:0073040584"} ! epidermal scale
property_value: editor_note "Currently being reviewed by PHENOSCAPE:ad" xsd:string
property_value: terminology_notes "we synonymize osteoderm and osteoscute with scute, see https://github.com/obophenotype/uberon/issues/516" xsd:string

