[Term]
id: UBERON:0008297
name: pennaceous feather
namespace: uberon
def: "Pennaceous feathers are also known as contour feathers. This type of feather is present in most modern birds, and has been shown in some species of maniraptoran dinosaurs. Pennaceous feathers have a central shaft (or rachis) with vanes spreading to either side. These vanes are composed of a high number of flattened barbs, that are connected to one another with barbules. The barbules are tiny strands that criss-cross on the flattened side s of the barbs. This forms a kind of miniature velcro-like mesh that holds all the barbs together, stabilizing the vanes. Flight feathers (remiges and rectrices) are specialized types of pennaceous feathers, adapted for high loadings and often strongly asymmetric for improved flight performance[WP]." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pennaceous_feather]
synonym: "contour feather" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pennaceous_feather]
synonym: "pennaceous" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pennaceous_feather]
xref: Pennaceous:feather
is_a: UBERON:0008292 ! vaned feather

[Term]
id: UBERON:0008304
name: inner chondrogenic layer of perichondrium
namespace: uberon
def: "The perichondrium consists of two separate layers: an outer fibrous layer and inner chondrogenic layer. The chondrogenic layer remains undifferentiated and can form chondroblasts or chondrocytes." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Free_Encyclopedia]
xref: BTO:0005090
xref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Free_Encyclopedia
relationship: part_of UBERON:0002222 {source="BTO"} ! perichondrium

[Term]
